TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "shaper (structural role: low usage, positive residual structural influence)",
    "cut-heavy initiator (high share of possessions initiated via CUT play type)",
    "drive-heavy initiator (high share via DRIVE play type)",
    "role portability / travel (stability of structural influence or displacement after a team change)",
    "play-type mix and structural fingerprint"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to threshold cut-heavy vs drive-heavy among shapers given continuous play-type shares?",
    "What metric best captures role travel: change in structural influence L, displacement D_real, or something else?",
    "Can cut and drive play-type labels be reliably assigned from play-by-play data?",
    "Is the n=24 unit pool sufficient to detect differences between two small, imbalanced subgroups?",
    "How to control for confounding by team system, usage change, age, and player-market endogenous moves?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No prior project records were returned; the retrieval yields zero evidence relevant to the question."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No earlier studies establish whether cut-heavy or drive-heavy shapers travel differently, nor any baseline role-portability gradient by play-type orientation.",
    "The feasibility, power, and typical effect sizes for such a comparison are unknown from earlier work."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Identifying shapers (already a small subset of players) and further splitting them into cut-heavy and drive-heavy shrinks the effective sample well below 24, likely to single-digit per group.",
    "CUT initiations are often off-ball, highly dependent on system, screening, and the primary creator – so a 'cut-heavy shaper' may simply reflect a coach’s scheme rather than a portable individual skill set.",
    "DRIVE initiations are more on-ball and may be similarly context-dependent (spacing, roll gravity, defensive scheme), so neither side has an obvious portability advantage absent data.",
    "The travel metric (change in structural influence or displacement) adds noise from roster changes, regression to the mean, and seasonal aging, further eroding power."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact definition of n=24 units (all shaper movers? only those meeting minimum-game criteria?) and the resulting subsample sizes.",
    "Reliability of CUT classification relative to DRIVE; misclassification could blur any genuine signal.",
    "Whether 'travels better' means role stability, less influence decline, or team adaptability, and how that maps onto existing metrics.",
    "Generalizability of any finding beyond the 2022-23 to 2025-26 window."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "The retrieval returned zero records; the decision relies solely on data-availability, design, and risk assessment."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.15,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Total unit count 24, with subgroups for cut-heavy and drive-heavy likely <12 each, provides very low power for any realistic between-group comparison."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Cut-heavy shapers may be extremely rare; a viable split may yield groups too small for a meaningful test."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "CUT play-type classification is less reliable than on-ball types; noise in the independent variable attenuates any true difference."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Team style, coaching, and teammate quality confound play-type skew and role travel; without strong instruments or exogenous moves, causal attribution is tenuous."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "Shapers defined by positive residual structural influence may regress toward the mean after a move, mimicking poor travel and potentially affecting groups differently."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No earlier project work retrieved that addresses cut- vs drive-heavy role portability."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Total sample of 24 shaper movers yields very low power for comparing two subgroups; minimum detectable effect exceeds plausible real-world differences."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Cut-heavy shapers are likely few, making one or both groups too sparse for reliable inference."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Play-by-play inferred CUT labels, especially off-ball actions, have non-trivial misclassification rates that add noise."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Team context determines both play-type opportunities and measured structural influence, making it difficult to isolate player-level portability."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Using residual-based shaper classification risks regression to the mean after a team change, distorting portability measures."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Even if a single test is pre-specified, the many researcher degrees of freedom (threshold choice, metric selection, filter criteria) inflate false positives without clear pre-registration."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.1,
    "untestable": 0.15,
    "artifact": 0.05,
    "uncertain": 0.65
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "Difference in role portability (change in structural influence load L or displacement D_real from pre-move to post-move season) between cut-heavy and drive-heavy shapers.",
    "unit": "player-season move (one observation per player per team change, using the first post-move season as outcome).",
    "sample": "Players classified as Shapers (below rotation-median usage, positive residual structural influence) in either pre-move or post-move season, with a team change between the 2022-23 and 2025-26 seasons, >30 games played in each season, >50 total initiations per season. Further subset into cut-heavy (>40% of initiations from CUT) and drive-heavy (>40% from DRIVE); exclude players meeting neither threshold.",
    "power": "With expected n per group ≤10, power for Cohen's d = 0.5 is around 15–25%; only extremely large effects (d ≈ 1.2) reach 80% power. Adequate power would require inflating the sample by pooling many more seasons or relaxing Shaper definition, which risks measurement validity.",
    "controls": "Age (season age), change in usage rate, change in team offensive rating (to proxy system strength), and pre-move residual structural influence to partially address RTM.",
    "falsification": "Apply the same portability metric to non-mover 'decoy' shapers matched on play-type mix and team strength; test whether cut-heavy vs drive-heavy show differential within-player season-to-season stability. A genuine portability effect should be absent in non-movers.",
    "multiplicity": "Only one primary confirmatory test (difference in mean portability between the two groups). No post-hoc subgroup analyses.",
    "decision_rule": "Two-sided Welch's t-test at alpha = 0.05; effect size reported as Cohen's d with 95% CI; sensitivity analyses varying the cut/drive threshold (30%, 35%, 50%) and excluding players near the boundary to check robustness.",
    "data_version": "NBA play-by-play and derived initiation records from the project's graph database for the 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 seasons, using the standard play-type classifier and structural-influence pipeline as of August 2026."
  }
}}

